# Synopsis

Manage session hooks: shell scripts that run on session events, or
explore the installed events and their documents.

# Events

A hookable event is a lowercase dot-separated name, e.g.
"state.read-loop.enter". Events are installed by the framework and by
third parties; "create" requires the event to be installed. The events
form a namespace tree: entries ending with "." are namespaces, other
entries are events.
An event or namespace entry may hold a "SUMMARY" file with a short
description; its content is the summary, taken verbatim (including
trailing newlines).

# Operations

- "create": create a hook for an event (not a namespace); discover the
  installed events with "list-events". Requires
  "source". "mode" selects
  how the hook content is provided: "command" (source is the script
  text), "copy" (source is a file whose content is copied: a snapshot
  taken at creation time), "symlink" (source is a file the hook links
  to: it follows later changes to the file). "name" has the form
  "<base>.<type>": the type suffix selects the run policy ("oneshot"
  runs once then is removed, "always" runs every time; other types are
  accepted and currently behave like "always", but do not rely on
  that); "name" is
  optional (an auto-generated "oneshot" name is used when omitted; a
  name of the form ".<type>" uses an auto-generated name with the
  given type).
  "append" (requires "name") appends to the hook with the same name,
  creating it if it does not exist, instead of failing with "hook
  already exists". The result reports the hook as "event/base.type".
- "remove": remove a hook. Requires "event" and "name" (with the exact
  type suffix of the installed hook).
- "list": list the hooks currently installed in the session. "pattern"
  can be a POSIX ERE searched within the "event/base.type" hook paths
  (all of them when omitted). Each entry reports "kind": "regular" or
  "symlink".
- "list-events": list the installed events and namespaces (namespace
  names end with "."). "pattern" can be a POSIX ERE searched within
  the listed names (all of them when "pattern" is omitted; the names
  include the trailing "." of namespaces); "with_summary" adds each
  item's summary.
- "list-docs": list the documents of an entry (an event or a namespace)
  and of its ancestors. Requires "entry" (an event or a namespace);
  "pattern" can be a POSIX ERE searched within the full
  "<entry name>/<path>" strings as listed. An entry that is not
  installed is an error.
- "doc": read documents. "docpaths" is a document path or a list of
  them (the paths reported by "list-docs"; at least one is required,
  and an empty list is an error); documents that do not exist, and
  paths whose entry is not installed, are silently omitted.
- "hook-dump": write the hook's script content to the file named by
  "output_file", which must not exist yet (its directory must exist).
  The result's "count" is the script's line count. Requires "event",
  "name" and "output_file".
- "hook-source": print the source file of a "symlink" hook. Requires
  "event" and "name"; the hook must be a symlink (an error otherwise).
  The source is an absolute pathname.
- "log-dump": write the hook log lines within which "pattern" (a
  POSIX ERE) is searched (all lines when "pattern" is omitted) to the
  file named by "output_file", which must not exist
  yet (its directory must exist). The lines start with a
  "[<event>/<hook name>] " prefix, so anchoring the pattern with "^\\["
  filters by it. Lines without a match produce an
  empty dump, not an error.
  Requires "output_file".

A document path has the form "<entry name>/<doc path relative to the
entry>", e.g. "builtin.state./STATES.md".

# Approval

"create" and "remove" require the user's approval. In non-interactive
sessions they are rejected unless automatic approval is configured.

# Returns

A JSON object. On success: create returns {"created":"<rel>"}
({"appended":"<rel>"} when appending); remove returns
{"removed":"<rel>"}; list returns
{"hooks":[{"kind":...,"rel":...},...]};
list-events returns {"events":[{"name":...,"summary":...},...]};
list-docs returns {"entry":...,"doclist":[...]}; doc returns
{"docs":[{"path":...,"content":...},...]}; hook-dump and log-dump return
{"dump":{"path":...,"count":...}}; hook-source returns {"source":...}.
On failure: {"error": <reason>}; when rejected: {"rejected": <reason>}.

<rel> is the relative hook path in the form event/base.type.

# Notes

Parameters that do not apply to the requested operation are silently
ignored. The "pattern" EREs are searched unanchored within the
matched text; anchor them with "^" and "$" to require a full match.

Use this tool to install scripts that run automatically on session
events (for example desktop notifications or log appends), not to run
commands directly; use the shell tool for that. Use "list-events" to
discover the installed events, and "list-docs" or "doc" to read their
documents; read the documents of an event before creating a hook for
it, as they may describe its execution environment. A hook's standard
output goes to the user's terminal, not to the model; its standard
error is appended to the hook log, which "log-dump" can filter into a
file. To observe a hook's result, have it write to the hook log
("llmcc_hook_log" or standard error) and read the dump back. The hook
log can grow; read the dump with tail or grep.
